 sides lie on grid lines, and no two tiles may overlap.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound – a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) is the set of squares to the **left** of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\);  \n\\(R_i\\) is the set of squares to the **right** of that uncovered square.\n\nIf \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) then \\(L_i=\\varnothing\\); if \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) then \\(R_i=\\varnothing\\).\n\nFor every non‑empty \\(L_i\\) place a single tile covering exactly the rectangle  \n\n\\[\n\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\\}=L_i .\n\\]\n\nFor every non‑empty \\(R_i\\) place a single tile covering the rectangle  \n\n\\[\n\\{i\\}\\times\\{\\sigma(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}=R_i .\n\\]\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint (they lie in distinct rows) and together cover all squares of the board except the uncovered set \\(U\\).  \n\nSince exactly one row contains \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) and exactly one row contains \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\), the total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{i\\mid L_i\\neq\\varnothing\\}\\bigr|\n   +\\bigl|\\{i\\mid R_i\\neq\\varnothing\\}\\bigr|\n   =(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles always exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nFor each uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) consider the two unit squares that are **adjacent** to it horizontally:\n\n* the *left neighbour* \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) if \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\);\n* the *right neighbour* \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) if \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\).\n\nBecause each column contains exactly one uncovered square, there is precisely **one** row whose uncovered square lies in column 1, and precisely **one** row whose uncovered square lies in column \\(n\\). Consequently the total number of such neighbours is  \n\n\\[\n( n-1 ) + ( n-1 ) = 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nCall these \\(2n-2\\) squares the *boundary squares*.\n\n---\n\n#### Claim  \nNo rectangular tile can contain two distinct boundary squares.\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nAssume a tile \\(T\\) contains two different boundary squares.  \nBecause the tile is a rectangle, the set of its columns is a consecutive interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) and the set of its rows is a consecutive interval \\([r_1,r_2]\\).\n\nIf the two boundary squares lie in the **same row** \\(i\\), then they are situated on opposite sides of the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\); the interval of columns \\([c_1,c_2]\\) would have to contain both \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\), and therefore also \\(\\sigma(i)\\) itself.  Hence the uncovered square would belong to \\(T\\), contradicting the requirement that uncovered squares are not covered.\n\nIf the two boundary squares lie in **different rows**, say \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) and \\((j,\\sigma(j)+1)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\), then the column interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) must contain both \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)+1\\).  Since the column indices are integers, the interval also contains every integer between them, in particular the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) (or \\(\\sigma(j)\\)).  Because the row interval \\([r_1,r_2]\\) contains both rows \\(i\\) and \\(j\\), the square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) (or \\((j,\\sigma(j))\\)) lies inside the rectangle, again contradicting the definition of a tile. ∎\n\n---\n\nBecause each tile can cover **at most one** boundary square, at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are required to cover all boundary squares.  Since every tile must cover at least one square (otherwise it would be superfluous), the total number of tiles in any admissible tiling is **≥ \\(2n-2\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion  \n\nThe construction of Section 1 shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient, and the argument of Section 2 p