ng can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles, and the explicit construction shows that \\(n\\) tiles are sufficient.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(n=2025\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025,\\qquad\nJ=\\begin{pmatrix}1&\\dots &1\\\\ \\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\ 1&\\dots &1\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times n},\n\\]\n\nand let \\(\\pi\\) be a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the entries of the permutation matrix  \n\n\\[\nP_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if }j=\\pi(i),\\\\[2pt]\n0 & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered; therefore the matrix that has to be covered is  \n\n\\[\nM=J-P .\n\\]\n\nA rectangular tile whose sides lie on grid lines and which does not contain any uncovered square corresponds exactly to a **combinatorial rectangle of 1\u2019s** in \\(M\\): a pair of index sets \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (rows) and \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (columns) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\forall i\\in R,\\; \\forall j\\in C:\\; M_{ij}=1,\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\((i,\\pi(i))\\notin R\\times C\\) for every \\(i\\in R\\).  \nThus the problem asks for the smallest number of such rectangles whose union is the set of all 1\u2011entries of \\(M\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound via rank  \n\nFor any \\(0\\!-\\!1\\) matrix \\(A\\), the **Boolean rank** \\(\\operatorname{brank}(A)\\) is the smallest number of monochromatic 1\u2011rectangles needed to cover all 1\u2011entries of \\(A\\).  \nA standard linear\u2011algebraic fact (see e.g.\u202fGraham\u2013Pollak, \u201cOn the structure of finite graphs\u201d) is  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{brank}(A)\\;\\ge\\;\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(A).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, each rectangle \\(R\\times C\\) can be written as the outer product of the characteristic vectors of \\(R\\) and \\(C\\); a sum of \\(k\\) such outer products yields a matrix of real rank at most \\(k\\).  Hence any representation of \\(A\\) as a sum of \\(k\\) rectangles forces \\(\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(A)\\le k\\), which is (1).\n\nNow compute the real rank of \\(M=J-P\\).\n\n* The all\u2011ones matrix \\(J\\) has rank \\(1\\) (its column space is spanned by the vector \\((1,\\dots ,1)^{\\!\\top}\\)).  \n* A permutation matrix \\(P\\) is a product of a permutation of the standard basis; consequently it is invertible, so \\(\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(P)=n\\).  \n\nSince \\(P\\) is invertible, the matrices \\(J\\) and \\(P\\) are linearly independent; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}(J-P)=n .\n\\]\n\nApplying (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{brank}(M)\\ge n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause each tile corresponds to a 1\u2011rectangle, (2) tells us that **any tiling must use at least \\(n\\) tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling that uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nWe now exhibit a tiling with \\(n\\) tiles, proving that the lower bound (2) is tight.\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k = \\bigl(\\{k,k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\bigr)\\times\\bigl(\\{1,2,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInterpretation of \\(T_k\\):\n\n* Its row set consists of all rows from \\(k\\) down to the bottom row \\(n\\).  \n* Its column set consists of all columns strictly **to the left** of column \\(k\\).  \n\nBecause the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) lies at column \\(i\\), the rectangle \\(T_k\\) never contains a diagonal cell:\n\n* If a row \\(i\\) belongs to the row set of \\(T_k\\) then \\(i\\ge k\\).  \n* The column set of \\(T_k\\) contains only columns \\(<k\\).  \n* Hence column \\(i\\) (the diagonal column of row \\(i\\)) is **not** in the column set, so \\((i,i)\\notin T_k\\).\n\nConsequently every cell of \\(T_k\\) is a 1\u2011entry of \\(M\\), i.e. it may be covered by a tile.\n\n**Disjointness and completeness.**  \nFor two distinct indices \\(k<\\ell\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{rows}(T_k) &= \\{k,\\dots ,n\\}, &\n\\text{cols}(T_k) &= \\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\\\\\n\\text{rows}(T_\\ell) &= \\{\\ell,\\dots ,n\\}, &\n\\text{cols}(T_\\ell) &= \\{1,\\dots ,\\ell-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\text{rows}(T_k)\\supseteq \\text{rows}(T_\\ell)\\) and \\(\\text{cols}(T_k)\\subseteq \\text{cols}(T_\\ell)\\).  \nThe two rectangles intersect only on the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,\\ell,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is empty because \\(k-1<\\ell\\).  Hence the rectangles \\(T_1,T_2,\\dots ,T_n\\) are pairwise **disjoint**.\n\nNow consider any off\u2011diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n* If \\(j<i\\) (the cell lies left of the diagonal), set \\(k=j+1\\).  \n  Then \\(i\\ge k\\) and \\(j\\le k-1\\), so \\((i,j)\\in T_k\\).\n* If \\(j>i\\) (the cell lies above the diagonal), set \\(k=i\\).  \n  Then \\(i\\ge k\\) and \\(j\\ge k+1\\); because the column set of \\(T_k\\) stops at \\(k-1\\), the cell is not in \\(T_k\\).  \n  However it **is** in \\(T_{j}\\): indeed \\(j\\ge j\\) and \\(i\\le j-1\\), so \\((i,j)\\in T_{j}\\).\n\nThus every off\u2011diagonal cell belongs to exactly one of the rectangles \\(T_k\\).  The diagonal cells are omitted, as required.\n\nSince we have constructed \\(n\\) rectangles that cover precisely the set of cells that must be tiled, we obtain a tiling with **\\(n\\) tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality  \n\nFrom Section\u202f1 we know that any tiling needs at least \\(n\\) tiles.  \nSection\u202f2 provides a tiling that uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the lower bound is attained and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(n\\).\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\) we conclude\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved: the smallest number of rectangular tiles that can be placed on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid so that each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square is \\(2025\\).**"
