 \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nEvery square \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r\\neq c\\) belongs either to the unique horizontal tile of its row (if \\(c>r\\)) or to the unique vertical tile of its column (if \\(c<r\\)).  \nNo tile contains a diagonal square, and the tiles are pairwise disjoint.  \nHence \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFix the permutation \\(\\pi\\).  \nFor a column \\(c\\) define the two row sets  \n\n\\[\nL_c=\\{\\,i\\mid \\pi(i)>c\\,\\},\\qquad\nR_c=\\{\\,i\\mid \\pi(i)<c\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Interpretation.*  \nIf a tile uses column \\(c\\) and also contains a square of row \\(i\\), then the tile must lie **entirely** on the side of the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nConsequently the rows of such a tile are a subset of \\(L_c\\) (the tile is to the left of the uncovered square of each of its rows) or a subset of \\(R_c\\) (the tile is to the right).\n\nBecause a tile is a rectangle, the set of its rows is an **interval** of consecutive integers.  \nHence a tile that uses column \\(c\\) occupies a *contiguous* block of rows inside one of the two sets \\(L_c\\) or \\(R_c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  Components forced on the left side  \n\nConsider the family \\(\\{L_c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-1\\}\\).  \nFor each \\(c\\) let \\(\\kappa_c\\) be the number of *connected components* (maximal intervals) of the set \\(L_c\\).  \nA component of \\(L_c\\) cannot be covered by a single tile together with another component, because any tile that contains rows from two distinct components would have to contain a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)=c\\); that row’s uncovered square lies exactly in column \\(c\\), contradicting the definition of a left‑side tile.  \nTherefore **each component of \\(L_c\\) forces a distinct vertical tile** that uses column \\(c\\).\n\nWe now estimate \\(\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}\\kappa_c\\).\n\nObserve that \\(L_{c+1}=L_c\\setminus\\{\\,i\\mid \\pi(i)=c+1\\,\\}\\).  \nRemoving the rows whose uncovered square is in column \\(c+1\\) can split at most one existing component of \\(L_c\\) into two, but it can also simply delete a row without creating a new component.  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\kappa_{c+1}\\ge \\kappa_c-1\\qquad (1\\le c\\le n-2).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(L_1=\\{\\,i\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\,\\}\\) is non‑empty, it contains at least one component, i.e. \\(\\kappa_1\\ge 1\\).  \nIterating the inequality gives  \n\n\\[\n\\kappa_{c}\\ge 1\\quad\\text{for every }c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}\\kappa_c\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBy the argument above, at least \\(\\kappa_c\\) distinct tiles are needed for column \\(c\\); summing over all columns yields that **at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are necessary on the left side** of the uncovered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Components forced on the right side  \n\nThe same reasoning applied to the sets \\(R_c\\) (rows whose uncovered square lies to the right of column \\(c\\)) shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles are required on the right side.  \nIndeed \\(R_{c+1}=R_c\\cup\\{\\,i\\mid \\pi(i)=c+1\\,\\}\\); the number of components of \\(R_c\\) can increase by at most one when passing from \\(c\\) to \\(c+1\\), and \\(R_{n}= \\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) has exactly one component.  \nRunning the argument backwards yields again a total of at least \\(n-1\\) components, and therefore at least \\(n-1\\) distinct tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  Total lower bound  \n\nCombining the left‑side and right‑side estimates we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{(number of tiles)}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom Section&nbsp;2 we have a construction using exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nFrom Section&nbsp;3 we have proved that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient.  \n\n∎"
