tually H_i occupies row i, columns > i. V_i occupies column i, rows > i. So they intersect only at cell (i,i)? No, H_i is row i, columns i+1..n; V_i is column i, rows i+1..n. So they intersect at (i,i) which is not covered because H_i doesn't include column i, V_i doesn't include row i. So they are disjoint. Also H_i and V_j for i≠j: H_i is row i, columns > i; V_j is column j, rows > j. Intersection would be at (i,j) if i>j and j>i? Actually for H_i and V_j to intersect, we need row i = some row in V_j's rows (i > j) and column j = some column in H_i's columns (j > i). Both can't hold simultaneously. So they are disjoint. So the construction is valid.\n\nThus the construction yields 2n-2 tiles covering all cells except the diagonal. So it's a feasible solution with 2n-2 tiles.\n\nNow the lower bound: They argue any tiling must have at least 2n-2 tiles. They use permutation representation of uncovered squares, define L_c and R_c sets, prove Lemma 1 about tile rows being within L_c or R_c for a given column c. Then define components of L_c and R_c, and argue each component requires a distinct tile. Then they sum components across columns to get at least n-1 for each side, total 2n-2.\n\nWe need to verify if this reasoning is valid. It seems plausible. Let's examine each step.\n\n- Represent uncovered squares as a permutation π: each row i has uncovered column π(i). Indeed each row has exactly one uncovered square, and each column also has exactly one uncovered square, so the mapping i → π(i) is a bijection, i.e., a permutation.\n\n- For a fixed column c, define L_c = {i | π(i) > c} (rows whose uncovered square is to the right of column c) and R_c = {i | π(i) c} (rows whose uncovered square is to the left of column c). This partitions rows except those with π(i) = c (the row where uncovered square is exactly at column c). Those rows are not in L_c or R_c.\n\n- Lemma 1: Any tile that uses column c is completely contained either in rows L_c or rows R_c. Proof: Suppose tile T = R × C (R is set of rows, C set of columns) contains column c (i.e., c ∈ C). If T contains rows i ∈ L_c and j ∈ R_c, then because R is an interval (tiles are rectangular, so rows are contiguous), all rows between i and j are also in R. Since π is a permutation, there is a row k between i and j such that π(k) = c (by pigeonhole principle). Then uncovered square (k,c) lies inside T, contradiction. So tile cannot contain rows from both L_c and R_c. So tile's rows are either all in L_c or all in R_c. This seems correct.\n\n- Consequently, rows of a tile that uses column c form a contiguous block (interval) inside either L_c or R_c. That's true because tile rows are contiguous by rectangle shape.\n\n- Define component: maximal interval of consecutive integers in a set. Let κ_c be number of components of L_c, λ_c number of components of R_c.\n\n- Lemma 2: At least κ_c distinct tiles are needed to cover the cells of column c that lie in L_c, and at least λ_c distinct tiles for cells in R_c. Reason: Each component of L_c consists of rows that are pairwise adjacent, and all must be covered by tiles whose rows are subsets of that component (by Lemma 1). A single tile cannot cover two different components because its rows would be non-contiguous. So each component forces a different tile. This seems correct.\n\n- Counting components: For c = 1,..., n-1, L_c is non-empty (otherwise all π(i) ≤ c, impossible because π is a permutation onto {1,...,n}). When we increase c to c+1, we delete exactly those rows i with π(i) = c+1; this can reduce the number of components by at most one. Starting with c=1 we have κ_1 ≥ 1; after n-1 steps we reach empty set L_n. Therefore sum_{c=1}^{n-1} κ_c ≥ n-1. This is a classic argument: each time we delete a row, we might merge two components into one, reducing count by at most one. So sum of component counts across columns is at least n-1. This seems correct.\n\n- By symmetry, sum_{c=1}^{n-1} λ_c ≥ n-1.\n\n- Lemma 3: Every tile is of left type (its whole column interval lies strictly left of the uncovered square of each of its rows) or of right type (its whole column interval lies strictly right). Proof: If a tile contained rows on both sides of the uncovered squares, Lemma 1 would be violated. This is consistent: For any tile, for each row i it covers, the uncovered column π(i) is either > all columns of tile (tile left of uncovered) or all columns of tile (tile right of uncovered). Since tile cannot have rows on both sides, it must be consistent across rows: either all rows have uncovered squares to the right (tile left type) or all rows have uncovered squares to the left (tile right type). This seems correct.\n\n- Then they argue each left-type tile is counted exactly once in sum κ_c: counted for column equal to its rightmost column. Similarly each right-type tile counted exactly once in sum λ_c: counted for its leftmost column. So total number of tiles T ≥ sum κ_c + sum λ_c ≥ 2(n-1) = 2n-2. This seems correct.\n\nThus the lower bound matches the construction, establishing optimality.\n\nThus the solution appears correct, reasoning valid, and presentation is fairly complete. However, we need to check for any presentation issues: Are there any missing justifications? The solution uses lemmas and proofs, but some steps may be considered \"justification gaps\"? Let's see.\n\n- In Lemma 1, they use \"by the pigeon‑hole principle there is a row k between i and j with π(k)=c\". This is correct: since π is a permutation, the values π(i) for rows between i and j must include all columns between π(i) and π(j) inclusive? Actually they claim there is a row k between i and j with π(k)=c. Since i ∈ L_c (π(i) > c) and j ∈ R_c (π(j) c), we have π(i) > c > π(j). The rows between i and j (including endpoints) are a set of rows. The values π of those rows are a set of columns. Since π is a bijection, the values are distinct. Among those rows, the columns π(i), π(i+1), ..., π(j) (if ij) are a set of distinct integers 